          "content": "### Research context and question\n\nFive hypothetical marine animals A, B, C, D and E are scored for seven binary anatomical states. One state concerns appendage number, another developmental cleavage and five concern independent cellular and skeletal features. The task is to reconstruct plausible relationships without using living species as ancestors. These figures and specimen descriptions are fictional; they are provided only to support reasoning about data collection and interpretation. Formulate the observational question before calculating anything. Identify the experimental unit, which might be an embryo, tank, specimen, independently sampled site or recorded trial. If several subsamples come from one individual or enclosure, they do not become independent biological replicates merely because each produced a distinct number.\n\n### Competing explanations and predictions\n\nIn **tree reconstruction under conflicting characters**, propose explanations that disagree over a measurable prediction, and specify whether the design would justify a causal interpretation or only an association. If intervention would require animal ethics review, use recordings, existing datasets or simulation as the safer teaching alternative.\n\n### Protocol and operational definitions\n\nAssign each scored state to the branches that minimize changes, compare two equally parsimonious candidate trees, and then add an eighth trait that is known to be ecologically convergent. Repeat the comparison after removing that trait. Write a reproducible log identifying dates or developmental stages, treatment assignment, instruments, the person making observations and any missing data. Define the response variable before viewing group averages. A count of organisms differs from a fraction of area occupied; mass differs from weight; a rate must specify a time unit. Independent replication should use distinct biological units, not repeated readings of a single sample. A valid control must experience the same handling and observation procedures except for the variable under study.\n\n### Worked analytical interpretation\n\nRecord character definitions, unknown states and alternative state codings. Report changes in inferred tree topology and distinguish parsimony scores from statistical confidence. Show the denominator and time interval at every step. Consider a plot with raw observations, not just a mean; if the values are too few for reliable uncertainty estimation, describe that limitation explicitly. When a measurement is a proxy for another process, write down the additional assumptions required to make the connection. For example, observed feeding-particle disappearance does not imply assimilation, and a tracked displacement may underestimate the path actually traveled.\n\n### What the evidence does and does not show\n\nA newly added conspicuous trait may support the wrong grouping if shared habitat creates convergence; consistency across independent characters is more convincing than a single attractive reconstruction. Correlation, temporal sequence and intervention can support different strengths of causal inference. Reporting a quantitative effect without its scale, replicates or alternate explanations encourages false certainty. If the prediction fails, revisit possible measurement flaws and the biological mechanism independently instead of adding ad hoc exceptions. Never interpret the fabricated numerical examples in this investigation as findings about a named species or habitat.\n\n### Extension and reproducibility\n\nAs a methodological extension, ask how the result would differ if the most diagnostic character were missing in one specimen. Compare coding missing states as unknown with wrongly coding them as absent. Propose a second independent dataset and explain why adding more of the same biased data may not solve the problem. Prepare one figure caption that states the organism or model, response units, sample size and uncertainty type, plus one short limitations paragraph. Have two independent teams code the same characters without sharing their preferred tree, then reconcile disagreements before drawing evolutionary conclusions. The investigation is successful when another learner can reproduce the analysis and understand what it cannot establish, not merely when a preferred hypothesis receives a favorable number.",
